    (including one that logs the username at submit time)
  - [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m + [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m — a small Node entry point with a [38;5;153mgreet[39m util
  - [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m with no dependencies and no test setup

  [1mClassification: architectural.[0m Logging is a new subsystem — there's no logging layer here to modify, just scattered
  [38;5;153mconsole.*[39m calls, and "debug production issues" implies the logs have to get somewhere you can read them after the
  fact. That means a spec and a written design rather than me just editing files. I'll ask a few questions one at a
  time, propose approaches, then write a design doc for your review.

  [1mFirst fork: which app.[0m This repo has two unrelated entry points, and the answer changes nearly everything downstream.
  Browser-side logging is the hard case — a [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m in a user's browser is invisible to you in production, so useful
  browser logging needs a transport that ships records to a server you control, plus batching, failure handling, and a
  privacy review (that login form handles passwords). Node-side logging is the cheap case — structured JSON to stdout,
  and whatever runs the process collects it; no transport to build. Doing both means one shared core with two different
  sinks, which is more design but avoids two incompatible log formats later.

  Picking one now is cheap to revisit; picking "both" and building the shared abstraction is the part that's expensive
  to undo if you only ever needed one.

  My recommendation: the browser app, since that's where the production debugging gap actually is — the Node side
  already prints to stdout where a process supervisor can capture it.
